We show that $k=2n-2$ satisfies the requirement. Suppose that a happy configuration of rooks is given, and let the rooks be located at $(a_{1},b_{1}),\ldots,(a_{n},b_{n})$, where $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are the row and column of the $i$th rook. The rooks are located in the square $[1,n]\times[1,n]$ of the chessboard, and we want to find a $k\times k$ square within this square that does not contain any of the rooks.

For each $i=1,2,\ldots,n$, let $c_{i}=a_{i}+b_{i}$ and $d_{i}=a_{i}-b_{i}$. The set of $c_{i}$'s is a permutation of the set of $d_{i}$'s, which is also a permutation of the set of $a_{i}+b_{i}$'s. This is because if we consider the square of the chessboard rotated by $45^{\circ}$, the diagonals of the chessboard are horizontal and vertical lines, and the $c_{i}$'s and $d_{i}$'s are the coordinates of the rooks on these lines.

Now, let $c_{1}\leqslant c_{2}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant c_{n}$, and let $d_{1}\leqslant d_{2}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant d_{n}$. Note that $c_{1}+d_{1}=2a_{1}$ and $c_{n}+d_{n}=2a_{n}$, so the $a_{i}$'s are a permutation of the set $\left\{\frac{c_{1}+d_{1}}{2},\frac{c_{2}+d_{2}}{2},\ldots,\frac{c_{n}+d_{n}}{2}\right\}$. Thus, there are no rooks in the square $[1,n]\times[1,n]$ with $x+y\leqslant c_{n}$ and $x-y\leqslant d_{1}$.

The vertices of the square are $(1,1),\,(1,n),\,(n,1),\,(n,n)$, so the largest $k$ such that the square $[1,n]\times[1,n]$ contains a $k\times k$ square with $x+y\leqslant c_{n}$ and $x-y\leqslant d_{1}$ is $\max\{c_{n},-d_{1}\}-1$. It suffices to show that $\max\{c_{n},-d_{1}\}-1\geqslant 2n-2$. Since $c_{1}\leqslant c_{2}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant c_{n}$ and $d_{1}\leqslant d_{2}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant d_{n}$, we have $c_{1}+d_{1}\leqslant c_{2}+d_{2}\leqslant\cdots\leqslant c_{n}+d_{n}$, so the $a_{i}$'s are a permutation of the set $\left\{c_{1}+d_{1},\frac{c_{1}+d_{1}}{2}+\frac{c_{2}+d_{2}}{2},\ldots,\frac{c_{n-1}+d_{n-1}}{2}+\frac{c_{n}+d_{n}}{2},c_{n}+d_{n}\right\}$. Thus, $c_{1}+d_{1}\leqslant 2$ and $c_{n}+d_{n}\geqslant 2n$, so $\max\{c_{n},-d_{1}\}\geqslant 2n-1$, as desired.

